'''Burhan Wani''' (19 September 1994 – 8 July 2016) was the leader of [[Hizbul Mujahideen]], an [[Islamic terrorism|Islamist terrorist]] organization of the [[Kashmir conflict]].{{efn|Hizbul Mujahideen was designated as a [[List of designated terrorist groups|designated as a terrorist group]] by the [[United States]], [[European Union]], [[India]] and [[Canada]].<ref name="Most-wanted">{{cite news|url=http://www.thehindu.com/news/national/other-states/burhan-wani-kashmir-valleys-most-wanted-militant-commander-killed/article8824756.ece|title=Burhan Wani, Hizbul poster boy, killed in encounter|date=9 July 2016|work=The Hindu|access-date=9 July 2016}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=US Designates Hizbul Mujahideen as a Foreign Terrorist Group|url=https://thediplomat.com/2017/08/us-designates-hizbul-mujahideen-as-a-foreign-terrorist-group/|access-date=2021-05-04|website=thediplomat.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Nisar|first=Naqshab|date=2021-02-04|title=After US and EU, Canada designates Hizbul Mujahideen as terrorist group|url=https://www.ibtimes.co.in/after-us-eu-canada-designates-hizbul-mujaheedin-terrorist-group-832825|access-date=2021-05-04|website=ibtimes.co.in}}</ref>}} He had become a popular figure amongst the local [[Kashmiris|Kashmiri]] populace, having done so primarily through a strong [[social media]] presence, and was responsible for moulding the [[insurgency in Jammu and Kashmir]] into a youth-oriented movement. Wani was a militant leader and had reportedly recruited numerous foot-soldiers through his personal efforts.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-asia-india-36762043|title=Why the death of militant Burhan Wani has Kashmiris up in arms|first=Shujaat|last=Bukhari|publisher=BBC|date=11 July 2016|access-date=30 July 2016}}</ref>

As a militant leader, Wani was actively sought by [[Indian Armed Forces|Indian security forces]], who had imposed a bounty for his capture. He was located in a remote village in the [[Anantnag district]] of Jammu and Kashmir and subsequently killed in a firefight with Indian forces on 8 July 2016. Wani's killing sparked [[2016–2017 Kashmir unrest|massive protests]] across the entire [[Kashmir Valley]]<ref name="Most-wanted" /><ref>{{cite news|title=This militant was a folk hero on social media. Now his death has roiled Indian Kashmir.|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/worldviews/wp/2016/07/11/this-violent-militant-was-a-folk-hero-on-social-media-now-his-death-has-roiled-indian-kashmir/|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|access-date=19 August 2017|date=11 July 2016}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Arya |first=Divya |title=Postbox kashmir : Two Lives in Letters |publisher=Penguin Random House India Private Limited |others=Inc OverDrive |year=2021 |isbn=978-93-5492-049-3 |location=Noida, Uttar Pradesh |oclc=1290491980}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Doctors stage protest in J-K against civilian deaths in Kashmir unrest|access-date=17 August 2016|url=http://indianexpress.com/article/india/india-news-india/jammu-and-kashmir-burhan-wani-doctors-stage-protest-in-j-k-against-civilian-deaths-in-kashmir-unrest-2966015/|work=The Indian Express|quote=Kashmir Valley is on the boil since July 9, a day after Hizbul Mujahideen commander Burhan Wani was killed in an encounter with security forces in Anantnag district of south Kashmir. The widespread protests claimed the lives of 55 people and left over 6000 injured, with hundreds hit by pellets in their eyes as a result of which, doctors say, a number of youths have lost their eyesight.}}</ref> in what became the worst span of unrest in the region since the [[2010 Kashmir unrest|2010 unrest]].<ref>{{cite news|title=India's Modi lashes out at Pakistan, Pakistan hits back|url=http://uk.reuters.com/article/uk-india-anniversary-idUKKCN10Q0FG|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160815074421/http://uk.reuters.com/article/uk-india-anniversary-idUKKCN10Q0FG|url-status=dead|archive-date=15 August 2016|access-date=17 August 2016|work=Reuters|date=15 August 2016|quote=Modi met national party leaders on Friday to seek ways to end the worst unrest in Kashmir since 2010.}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Five civilians killed, 31 injured in fresh firing in Kashmir; toll reaches 65|url=http://www.hindustantimes.com/india-news/four-civilians-killed-in-firing-in-jammu-and-kashmir-s-budgam-district/story-N8ZhElghzojEGN94KNylCL.html|access-date=17 August 2016|work=Hindustan Times|date=17 August 2016|quote=The worst violence since 2010 – when the Valley was rocked by similar protests leaving scores dead and injured – has sparked a verbal spat between India and Pakistan, both blaming each other for the flare-up.}}</ref> As a result, the state of Jammu and Kashmir was placed under 53 consecutive days of curfew, which was fully lifted by 31 August 2016.<ref name="DH">{{cite news|title=Curfew lifted from Valley, one killed in clash in Sopore|url=http://www.deccanherald.com/content/567874/curfew-lifted-valley-one-killed.html|access-date=31 August 2016|agency=Press Trust of India|date=31 August 2016}}</ref><ref name="TH">{{cite news|title=One killed, 100 injured in Valle|url=http://www.thehindu.com/news/national/other-states/authorities-lift-curfew-in-kashmir-valley-as-clashes-erupt-in-some-places/article9054322.ece|author=Peerzada Ashiq|work=The Hindu|access-date=31 August 2016|date=31 August 2016}}</ref><ref name="GKCurfew">{{cite news|url=http://www.greaterkashmir.com/news/kashmir/story/227253.html|title=Curfew lifted from entire Kashmir valley, says Div Com|work=Greater Kashmir|date=31 August 2016|access-date=31 August 2016|archive-date=21 March 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190321142159/https://www.greaterkashmir.com/news/kashmir/story/227253.html|url-status=dead}}</ref> The protests that follow